      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed that SwapPss needs fixing for 4KB processes: at the parent, each of the four swap PTEs of a private 16KB entry adds `PAGE_SIZE / 4`, so SwapPss is a quarter of Swap.\n\nFor resident pages, `smaps_account()` already shares the 16KB page among all the PTEs that map it, capped at the 4KB that each one maps (aosp/4312510). Would the same work for swap, with `swp_swapcount()` as the count? Something like:\n\n```\nif (mapcount \u003e\u003d 2) {\n        u64 pss_delta \u003d (u64)PAGE_SIZE_KERNEL \u003c\u003c PSS_SHIFT;\n\n        do_div(pss_delta, mapcount);\n        mss-\u003eswap_pss +\u003d min(pss_delta, (u64)PAGE_SIZE \u003c\u003c PSS_SHIFT);\n}\n```\n\nThat needs no PTE scan or new helper, leaves native processes unchanged (`PAGE_SIZE` is `PAGE_SIZE_KERNEL` there), and a page then reports the same PSS whether it\u0027s resident or swapped out. It also gives a parent and child that keep disjoint slices (say 0-1 and 2-3) 8KB each, where the local count halves them. The single-slice case from the commit message would get 4KB in each process, as it does while resident.\n\nAt the top of the chain, aosp/4367962 switches resident anonymous pages to the fixed four, which runs into the single-slice problem this message describes, so I think keeping the formula of aosp/4312510 on both sides would be the most consistent.\n\nIf the scan stays, could this comment get a blank line before it?",
